{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-explainer-about-how-people-spend-time-in-autumn-in-japan-canada-germany-and-mexico-writ/audio/main-be09c9e86af2.mp3","durationSec":228.96,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.32,"text":"Autumn activities are easier to understand with three questions: When do they happen?"},{"start":4.86,"end":6.02,"text":"Where do people spend time?"},{"start":6.62,"end":7.94,"text":"What does the activity mean?"},{"start":9.06,"end":11.9,"text":"We can use these questions in all four countries."},{"start":12.64,"end":20.1,"text":"To see how people spend time in autumn in Japan, start with Tsukimi, the Japanese autumn moon-viewing custom."},{"start":20.1,"end":24.06,"text":"Jugoya is the fifteenth night of the eighth lunar month."},{"start":24.62,"end":27.36,"text":"In 2026, Tsukimi is on 25 September."},{"start":28.48,"end":33.92,"text":"People display dango, round rice dumplings, and susuki, or silver grass."},{"start":34.8,"end":37.08,"text":"The round dango stand for the full moon."},{"start":37.84,"end":40.14,"text":"The grass stands for rice and good crops."},{"start":41.34,"end":44.34,"text":"Now add place and daily routine to our three questions."},{"start":45.42,"end":48.2,"text":"Many people enjoy Tsukimi at home under the moonlight."},{"start":49.18,"end":51.78,"text":"It is a custom, not a national public holiday."},{"start":52.28,"end":56.86,"text":"There are no holiday days connected with the moon festival, so work continues as normal."},{"start":56.86,"end":58.9,"text":"Offices and shops stay open."},{"start":59.64,"end":62.56,"text":"Businesses may still mark the season with special foods and marketing."},{"start":63.12,"end":66.38,"text":"This shows how a meaningful custom can be part of an ordinary working day."},{"start":66.92,"end":70.74,"text":"People do not need a whole day away from work to notice the season and enjoy the moon."},{"start":71.76,"end":76.16,"text":"In Canada, people spend time in autumn looking at fall colour and marking Thanksgiving."},{"start":77.02,"end":79.34,"text":"Fall colour means leaves changing colour in autumn."},{"start":80.04,"end":84.68,"text":"Algonquin Park, a large provincial park in Ontario, is one place where people can see this change."},{"start":85.1,"end":92.06,"text":"The colour of Sugar Maple and Red Maple leaves is usually strongest in the last week of September or the first week of October."},{"start":92.86,"end":98.98,"text":"Canadian Thanksgiving is on the second Monday in October, and some park services stay open until that weekend."},{"start":99.5,"end":106.84,"text":"In our three-question lens, the place is a park, the time is early autumn, and the changing leaves are the main natural event."},{"start":107.76,"end":113.64,"text":"In Germany, people spend autumn days at Oktoberfest, Munich’s big autumn festival."},{"start":114.34,"end":115.9,"text":"Munich is a city in Bavaria."},{"start":116.44,"end":125.64,"text":"The 191st Oktoberfest will take place at Theresienwiese, the festival ground in Munich, from 19 September to 4 October 2026."},{"start":126.42,"end":128.32,"text":"This is a very large public event."},{"start":128.96,"end":131.86,"text":"About 6.5 million guests visited in 2025."},{"start":132.9,"end":138.98,"text":"Its history began with a royal marriage celebration on fields outside Munich on 12 October 1810."},{"start":139.88,"end":143.78,"text":"Horse races happened again the next year, and the event grew into Oktoberfest."},{"start":144.56,"end":148.82,"text":"Here, autumn time is spent in a busy public place rather than at home."},{"start":149.24,"end":156.06,"text":"In Mexico, people spend time in autumn preparing for Día de Muertos, the Mexican Day of the Dead festivity."},{"start":156.6,"end":160.1,"text":"It happens each year from the end of October to the beginning of November."},{"start":160.72,"end":163.74,"text":"It marks the short return of family members who have died."},{"start":163.74,"end":167.94,"text":"Families make an ofrenda, a home offering or shrine for the dead."},{"start":168.32,"end":174.3,"text":"They place favourite dishes, flowers, candles, and handmade objects around home shrines and tombs."},{"start":174.92,"end":177.22,"text":"Flower petals and candles can also mark paths."},{"start":178.04,"end":182,"text":"The period comes at the end of the yearly maize, or corn, growing cycle."},{"start":182.66,"end":187.08,"text":"UNESCO put this living tradition on its Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2008."},{"start":188.4,"end":190.66,"text":"Our three questions show important differences."},{"start":191.52,"end":195.16,"text":"In Japan, many people view the moon at home while normal work continues."},{"start":195.78,"end":201.28,"text":"In Canada, people look at changing leaves in places such as Algonquin Park and mark Thanksgiving."},{"start":201.84,"end":205.24,"text":"In Germany, people attend a large public festival in Munich."},{"start":206.02,"end":210.86,"text":"In Mexico, families prepare home shrines and tombs for relatives who have died."},{"start":211.6,"end":215.88,"text":"The places are different: a home, a park, a festival ground, or a tomb."},{"start":216.72,"end":220.52,"text":"The times are also different, from late September to early November."},{"start":221.2,"end":228.44,"text":"However, each activity gives people a way to notice the autumn season and spend time with a custom, place, or memory."}],"words":[{"text":"Autumn","start":0,"end":0.28},{"text":"activities","start":0.28,"end":0.74},{"text":"are","start":0.74,"end":1.08},{"text":"easier","start":1.08,"end":1.38},{"text":"to","start":1.38,"end":1.58},{"text":"understand","start":1.58,"end":2.02},{"text":"with","start":2.02,"end":2.24},{"text":"three","start":2.24,"end":2.56},{"text":"questions","start":2.56,"end":3.02},{"text":"When","start":3.62,"end":3.7},{"text":"do","start":3.7,"end":3.86},{"text":"they","start":3.86,"end":4},{"text":"happen","st